---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/apps/tinyemail';
const title = "tinyEmail for Shopify: a separate email workspace and catalog workflow";
const desc = "Assess product email production, Shopify audience mapping and automation acceptance before moving live email work.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is tinyEmail relevant on an SMS marketing site?",
    "answer": "Its documented email role is relevant when planning a separate email workspace alongside other channels. Confirm any required SMS capability with current documentation."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Shopify Entire Customer Base mean everyone should receive marketing?",
    "answer": "No. Treat audience synchronization and marketing eligibility as separate decisions and retain the applicable permission and suppression evidence."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should every existing flow move at once?",
    "answer": "Move a flow after its required data, content, eligibility and queued-action checks pass, with one active sending owner and a rollback plan."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">App directory</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Operating review · November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <article class="container prose-shopify pb-16">
    <h2>Where tinyEmail can help</h2>
    <p>tinyEmail offers a dedicated email workspace with templates, product blocks, workflows and segmentation on its <a class="link" href="https://www.tinyemail.com/shopify-email-marketing">Shopify product page</a>. It can suit a team that wants to assemble catalog-led campaigns and repeatable email journeys outside its current editor. The relevant decision is whether its production and data workflow fits your store.</p>
    <h2>Evaluate the documented email role</h2>
    <p>This review assesses email. The cited documentation does not establish an SMS implementation for this account; it also does not establish that every unlisted capability is absent. If SMS is part of the project, request current channel documentation and an account demonstration. Keep email and SMS permissions separate when mapping customer records.</p>
    <h2>Catalog blocks need a reviewable snapshot</h2>
    <p>Product blocks reduce manual assembly, but propose a fixture with a variant, a sale item, a sold-out item and a removed product. Compare the preview and delivered email against the intended image, price, currency and destination. Change a product after creating the draft and inspect whether the content refreshes or remains a snapshot. Decide who rechecks the final creative before release.</p>
    <h2>Know which Shopify audience you selected</h2>
    <p>The <a class="link" href="https://support.tinyemail.com/portal/en/kb/articles/shopify-audience-20-6-2025">audience sync documentation</a> distinguishes Shopify Accepts Marketing from Shopify Entire Customer Base. The latter filters certain suppressed contacts but should not be treated as a fresh permission grant. Record the intended audience and subscription evidence before enabling a campaign; an imported customer record is not by itself your send rule.</p>
    <h2>Proposed identity acceptance fixture</h2>
    <p>Create controlled records for an opted-in buyer, a buyer who declined marketing, an unsubscribed address and an address changed in Shopify. Inspect which destination record and segment each update reaches, including duplicate-email handling. Repeat an update to inspect retry behavior. Record expected inclusion and exclusion before checking actual results. These are suggested acceptance checks, not observations from a performed trial.</p>
    <h2>Map the actual automation you need</h2>
    <p>tinyEmail documents Shopify email automation in its <a class="link" href="https://support.tinyemail.com/portal/en/kb/articles/how-to-set-up-email-automation-for-shopify-21-5-2024-1">setup guide</a>. Inventory your required triggers, delays, exits and content before choosing replacements. Distinguish tinyEmail functionality from its companion tinyAlbert product and confirm the enabled account workflow. A familiar flow name does not prove identical enrollment or cancellation rules.</p>
    <h2>Proposed queued-flow acceptance fixture</h2>
    <p>For a welcome or cart workflow available in the account, inspect entry, then change the controlled profile before the next queued action: purchase, unsubscribe or update its address. Observe the queue and final eligibility without sending to real customers. Include repeated events and a reconnect after interrupted sync. Write down where failures appear and who resolves them before moving production traffic.</p>
    <h2>Use segments as explicit definitions</h2>
    <p>The <a class="link" href="https://support.tinyemail.com/portal/en/kb/articles/shopify-store-faqs-21-5-2024-1">Shopify FAQ</a> describes purchase-based segment fields. Build one useful audience, such as previous buyers of a product family who remain email eligible, and compare expected members with the account output. Specify how refunds, changed addresses and stale sync affect membership. Confirm each needed field rather than assuming every Shopify field is available.</p>
    <h2>Budget for a separate email operation</h2>
    <p>Check the current Shopify offer and account eligibility on the product page rather than relying on an old numeric tier. Include email volume, chargeable contacts, production time, integration work and temporary provider overlap. Compare cost per eligible assigned customer and contribution after discounts, refunds and variable order costs. Subtract app costs once; do not also subtract discounts already reflected in net sales. An attractive subscription does not measure the cost of maintaining two workflows.</p>
    <h2>Move flows by acceptance, not store revenue</h2>
    <p>Assign an owner for each email journey and preserve permission and suppression history. Approve sender identity, catalog fixtures and queued-flow behavior before activating replacements; retire the corresponding old queue to avoid duplicate sends. Keep rollback ownership and exports documented. Use <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">email–SMS coordination</a> for shared calendar decisions and the <a class="link" href="/apps">app directory</a> to compare other operating models.</p>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
